---
import Shell from '../layouts/Shell.astro';
import sources from '../data/sources.json';
import provenance from '../data/provenance.json';
const retrieved = new Date(provenance.retrievedAt).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' });
---
<Shell title="Sources & methodology" active="sources"><article class="prose"><span class="eyebrow">Follow the reference</span><h1>Sources & methodology</h1><p>Seerah Timeline places events from the life of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ in order and links each event to a named report or Quran passage.</p><h2>What this app quotes</h2><p>Quran text comes from <a href="https://mcp.quran.ai" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran Foundation’s official Quran MCP <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a> and is bundled with the app, so it works without a live connection.</p><ul><li>Arabic: Uthmani Hafs, <code>{provenance.quranEdition}</code>, preserved exactly as retrieved.</li><li>English: Marmaduke Pickthall, <code>{provenance.translationEdition}</code>. His historical public-domain wording is kept rather than silently modernised. A translation is an interpretation of meaning.</li></ul><p>Hadith references name the collection and report number on <a href="https://sunnah.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sunnah.com <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a> (for example Sahih al-Bukhari 3). Reports are summarised in our own words; modern English hadith translations are not republished. Grading labels identify the collection; the app does not regrade chains of narration.</p><h2>What is editorial</h2><p>Event titles, summaries and the grouping into periods are editorial. Date bands are approximate navigation aids: a report that supports an event does not necessarily establish the displayed CE year, and event notes say where placement is uncertain or disputed. None of it is a ruling or an opinion from quran.ai, Quran.com or Quran Foundation, and no independent scholarly review is claimed.</p><h2>Limits</h2><p>The timeline covers selected events of the prophetic mission. It is not an exhaustive biography.</p><h2>Snapshot and reuse</h2><p>Canonical snapshot retrieved on {retrieved}. Source records, checksums and edition names ship with the project. Original application code is MIT licensed. DM Sans, Newsreader and Amiri are hosted locally under the SIL Open Font License, with their licence files included.</p><p class="micro">Snapshot SHA-256: <code style="overflow-wrap:anywhere">{provenance.snapshotSha256}</code><br />App content SHA-256: <code style="overflow-wrap:anywhere">{provenance.appContentSha256}</code></p><h2>References used in this app</h2><p>{sources.length} linked sources. Each opens the source provider in a new tab.</p><ul class="source-list">{sources.map(source=><li><a href={source.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{source.label} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a> <small>{source.kind}{'note' in source && source.note ? ` · ${source.note}` : ''}</small></li>)}</ul><div class="actions top-gap"><a class="button" href="/">Return to the activity <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a> <button class="button secondary" data-print>Print source notes</button></div></article></Shell>
